      "id": 1,
      "text": "Rexford sprang to life in 1901 when the Great Northern Railway built a branch line from Jennings to Fernie, British Columbia.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19,
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rexford%2C_Montana"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Many historic Rexford buildings were moved to the Tobacco Valley Historical Village in Eureka, where they now form the village’s core.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://roamingmontana.com/rexford/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town’s original site lost most of its monuments to the rising waters of Lake Koocanusa, yet Rexford still offers a gateway to the region’s wonders.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://discoveringmontana.com/towns/rexford/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Rexford’s name traces back to the native Irish term ‘Kings Crossing,’ chosen by founder George M. to honor his heritage.",
